WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Ask what the response proves.

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:06.195
Detection means noticing that sound is present or absent.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:11.645
A child dropping a block when a bell rings demonstrates awareness, without having to name the sound.

00:00:12.475 --> 00:00:16.635
Discrimination means judging whether sounds are the same or different.

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:23.465
Identification means specifying which sound was heard, perhaps by pointing, repeating, or writing.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.010
Comprehension adds meaning.

00:00:26.535 --> 00:00:33.815
Recognizing a doorbell is identification; understanding that somebody may be waiting outside uses the meaning of that event.

00:00:34.535 --> 00:00:39.975
Similarly, repeating a direction does not by itself prove that the child understands it.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.930
Following a novel meaningful direction provides stronger evidence.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.910
The hierarchy guides training, but it is not a rigid schedule.

00:00:50.355 --> 00:00:54.755
A child may work on more than one level or return to an earlier skill.

00:00:55.505 --> 00:00:59.505
Always consider the actual task, rather than the game title.

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:05.185
Picture selection and a hand raise can look equally simple while demanding different auditory skills.

